<http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language/JPN> ccmm_props:ISO_639_1 "ja"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_1>; ccmm_props:ISO_639_2B "jpn"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_2B>; ccmm_props:ISO_639_2T "jpn"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_2T>; ccmm_props:ISO_639_3 "jpn"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3>; ccmm_props:XML_LNG "ja"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG>; a skos:Concept; skos:altLabel "nihongo"@ja, "日本語"@ja; skos:definition "Japanese is an East Asian language spoken primarily in Japan. It is a member of the Japonic (or Japanese-Ryukyuan) language family, whose relation to other language groups is debated. Japanese has no genetic relationship with Chinese, but it makes extensive use of Chinese characters, or kanji, in its writing system, and a large portion of its vocabulary is borrowed from Chinese. Along with kanji, the Japanese writing system primarily uses two syllabic (or moraic) scripts, hiragana and katakana. Latin script is used in a limited way, such as for imported acronyms, and the numeral system uses mostly Arabic numerals, alongside traditional Chinese numerals."@en, "Le japonais est la langue du Japon, parlée par le peuple japonais. Le japonais appartient à la famille isolée des langues japoniques. La langue japonaise utilise conjointement deux ensembles de caractères distincts: les kanjis, écriture logographique d’origine chinoise, et les kanas, système moraïque dérivé des kanjis. Les kanas se divisent eux-mêmes en deux groupes, les hiraganas et les katakanas. Les katakanas sont employés pour l’écriture de mots d’origine étrangère au Japon, et les hiraganas, pour noter la plupart des mots et affixes grammaticaux."@fr; skos:inScheme <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language>; skos:prefLabel "японски"@bg, "japonština"@cs, "japansk"@da, "Japanisch"@de, "ιαπωνικά"@el, "Japanese"@en, "japonés"@es, "jaapani keel"@et, "japani"@fi, "japonais"@fr, "Seapáinis"@ga, "japanski"@hr, "japanšćina"@hsb, "japán"@hu, "japanska"@is, "giapponese"@it, "japonų kalba"@lt, "japāņu valoda"@lv, "Јапонски"@mk, "Ġappuniż"@mt, "Japans"@nl, "japansk"@no, "japoński"@pl, "japonês"@pt, "japoneză"@ro, "японский"@ru, "japanski"@sh, "japončina"@sk, "japonščina"@sl, "Јапански"@sr, "japanska"@sv, "japonca"@tr, "японська мова"@uk .
<http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language/JPR> ccmm_props:ISO_639_2B "jpr"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_2B>; ccmm_props:ISO_639_2T "jpr"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_2T>; ccmm_props:ISO_639_3 "jpr"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3>; ccmm_props:XML_LNG "jpr"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG>; a skos:Concept; skos:altLabel "Fārsi"@jpr, "פרסית יהודית"@jpr; skos:definition "Judeo-Persian refers to both a group of Jewish dialects spoken by the Jews living in Iran and Judeo-Persian texts (written in Hebrew alphabet). As a collective term, Judeo-Persian refers to a number of Judeo-Iranian languages spoken by Jewish communities throughout the formerly extensive Persian Empire. The language is part of the Persian group of the southwestern family of the Indo-Iranian languages."@en; skos:inScheme <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language>; skos:prefLabel "юдеоперсийски"@bg, "judeo-perština"@cs, "judeopersisk"@da, "Judäo-Persisch"@de, "Ιουδαιοπερσικά"@el, "Judeo-Persian"@en, "judeo-persa"@es, "juudipärsia"@et, "juutalaispersia"@fi, "judéo-persan"@fr, "Peirsis Ghiúdach"@ga, "judeoperzijski"@hr, "judeo-perzsa"@hu, "giudeo-persiano"@it, "žydų persų"@lt, "ebreju persiešu valoda"@lv, "Ġudeo-Persjan"@mt, "Judeo-Perzisch"@nl, "judeo-perski"@pl, "persa judeu"@pt, "iudeo-persană"@ro, "judeoperzština"@sk, "judejska perzijščina"@sl, "judeopersiska"@sv .
