<http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language/LZH> ccmm_props:ISO_639_3 "lzh"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3>; ccmm_props:XML_LNG "lzh"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG>; a skos:Concept; skos:altLabel "Classical Chinese"@en, "wényán"@lzh, "文言"@lzh; skos:definition "Classical Chinese or Literary Chinese is the language in which the classics of Chinese literature were written, from c. the 5th century BC. For millennia thereafter, the written Chinese used in these works was imitated and iterated upon by scholars in a form now called Literary Chinese, which was used for almost all formal writing in China until the early 20th century. Each written character corresponds to a single spoken syllable, and almost always to a single independent word. As a result, the characteristic style of the language is comparatively terse."@en; skos:inScheme <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language>; skos:prefLabel "литературен китайски"@bg, "literární čínština"@cs, "litterær kinesisk"@da, "klassisches Chinesisch"@de, "Λόγια κινεζικά"@el, "Literary Chinese"@en, "chino literario"@es, "klassikaline hiina"@et, "klassinen kiina"@fi, "chinois littéraire"@fr, "Sínis Liteartha"@ga, "književni kineski"@hr, "irodalmi kínai"@hu, "cinese letterario"@it, "klasikinė kinų"@lt, "ķīniešu literārā valoda"@lv, "Ċiniż Letterarju"@mt, "Klassiek Chinees"@nl, "literacki chiński"@pl, "chinês literário"@pt, "chineză literară"@ro, "literárna čínština"@sk, "knjižna kitajščina"@sl, "klassisk kinesiska"@sv .
