<http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language/XBE> ccmm_props:ISO_639_3 "xbe"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3>; ccmm_props:XML_LNG "xbe"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG>; a skos:Concept; skos:altLabel "Pikumbul"@en, "Bigambal"@xbe; skos:definition "Bigambal is an extinct Australian Aboriginal language formerly spoken in Goondiwindi Region, Queensland. According to Glottolog, Bigambal is closely related to Yugambal. In any case, Bigambal belongs to the Pama–Nyungan family, the most widespread family of Australian Aboriginal languages, comprising over 300 of the approximately 400 Aboriginal languages of Australia."@en; skos:inScheme <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language>; skos:prefLabel "Bigambal"@en .
<http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language/XBG> ccmm_props:ISO_639_3 "xbg"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3>; ccmm_props:XML_LNG "xbg"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG>; a skos:Concept; skos:altLabel "Booandik-ngolo"@xbg, "drualat-ngolonung"@xbg; skos:definition "Bunganditj, also known as drualat-ngolonung, is a Pama-Nyungan language spoken in south-western Victoria, Australia. Together with the closely related Warrnambool dialect continuum, it forms the Warrnambool-Bunganditj group. The Pama–Nyungan languages are the most widespread family of Australian Aboriginal languages, comprising over 300 of the approximately 400 Aboriginal languages of Australia."@en; skos:inScheme <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language>; skos:prefLabel "Bunganditj"@en .
