<https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages/csv> ccmm_props:ISO_639_3 "csv"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3>; ccmm_props:XML_LNG "csv"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG>; a skos:Concept; skos:altLabel "Sumtu"@csv, "Sumtu"@en; skos:definition "Sumtu Chin is a Tibeto-Burman language falling under the Kuki-Chin sub-branch of the Sino-Tibetan language family. Sumtu is spoken in Ann, Minbya, and Myebon townships in Rakhine State, Myanmar. Compared to Sinitic languages, Kuki-Chin languages are tonal but less so, and they are less isolating and more morphologically structured. They also tend to display areal features with neighbouring Austro-Asiatic languages."@en; skos:exactMatch <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language/CSV>; skos:inScheme <https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages>; skos:prefLabel "Sumtu Chin"@en .
<https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages/csw> ccmm_props:ISO_639_3 "csw"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3>; ccmm_props:XML_LNG "csw"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG>; a skos:Concept; skos:altLabel "Omaškêkowak"@csw; skos:definition "Swampy Cree (variously known as Maskekon, Maskegon and Omaškêkowak, and often anglicized as Omushkego) is a variety of the Cree macrolanguage. It is spoken in a series of Swampy Cree communities in northern Manitoba, central northeast of Saskatchewan along the Saskatchewan River and along the Hudson Bay coast and adjacent inland areas to the south and west, and Ontario along the coast of Hudson Bay and James Bay. Like other Cree varieties, Moose Cree is classified under the Algonquian branch of the Algic language family."@en; skos:exactMatch <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language/CSW>; skos:inScheme <https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages>; skos:prefLabel "Swampy Cree"@en .
