<https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages/kiq> ccmm_props:ISO_639_3 "kiq"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3>; ccmm_props:XML_LNG "kiq"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG>; a skos:Concept; skos:altLabel "Kosare"@en, "Kosadle"@kiq; skos:definition "Kosadle is a Papuan language of West Papua. Kosadle (Kosare) and Kaure are clearly related languages, but their relation to other Papuan languages has not been clarified."@en; skos:exactMatch <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language/KIQ>; skos:inScheme <https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages>; skos:prefLabel "Kosadle"@en .
<https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages/kir> ccmm_props:ISO_639_1 "ky"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_1>; ccmm_props:ISO_639_2B "kir"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_2B>; ccmm_props:ISO_639_2T "kir"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_2T>; ccmm_props:ISO_639_3 "kir"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3>; ccmm_props:XML_LNG "ky"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG>; a skos:Concept; skos:altLabel "kirghize"@fr, "kırgızça"@ky, "кыргызча"@ky; skos:definition "Kyrgyz is a Turkic language spoken in Kyrgyzstan as well as China, Afghanistan, Kazakhstan, Tajikistan, Turkey, Uzbekistan, Pakistan and Russia. Kyrgyz is a member of the Kyrgyz–Kipchak subgroup of the Kypchak languages, and modern-day language convergence has resulted in a mutual intelligibility between Kyrgyz and Kazakh.In 1940 a Cyrillic alphabet eventually became common and has remained so to this day, though some Kyrgyz still use the Arabic alphabet. There was a popular idea among some Kyrgyz people to make a transition to the Latin alphabet, but the plan has not been implemented yet."@en, "Le kirghiz ou kirghize est une langue appartenant au groupe des langues turques de la famille des langues altaïques. Il est parlé en Asie centrale, principalement au Kirghizstan (où il est la langue nationale), au Tadjikistan, au Xinjiang et en Afghanistan.On l’écrit au moyen de l’alphabet cyrillique, auquel trois lettres sont ajoutées, mais le kirghiz est parfois écrit en utilisant l’alphabet arabe, lui aussi complété. Le kirghiz est très proche du kazakh. Il est habituellement classé avec celui-ci ainsi que le tatar et le karakalpak au sein du groupe des langues kiptchak (ou branche turque occidentale)."@fr; skos:exactMatch <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language/KIR>; skos:inScheme <https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages>; skos:prefLabel "киргизки"@bg, "kyrgyzština"@cs, "kirgisisk"@da, "Kirgisisch"@de, "κιργιζικά"@el, "Kyrgyz"@en, "kirguís"@es, "kirgiisi keel"@et, "kirgiisi"@fi, "kirghiz"@fr, "Cirgis"@ga, "kirgiški"@hr, "kirgiščina"@hsb, "kirgiz"@hu, "kirgisíska"@is, "kirghiso"@it, "kirgizų kalba"@lt, "kirgīzu valoda"@lv, "киргиски"@mk, "Kirgiż"@mt, "Kirgizisch"@nl, "kirgisisk"@no, "kirgiski"@pl, "quirguiz"@pt, "kârgâză"@ro, "киргизский"@ru, "kirgiški"@sh, "kirgizština"@sk, "kirgiščina"@sl, "кыргыз"@sr, "kirgiziska"@sv, "kırgızca"@tr, "киргизька мова"@uk .
