<https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages/tgj> ccmm_props:ISO_639_3 "tgj"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3>; ccmm_props:XML_LNG "tgj"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG>; a skos:Concept; skos:exactMatch <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language/TGJ>; skos:inScheme <https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages>; skos:prefLabel "Tagin"@en .
<https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages/tgk> ccmm_props:ISO_639_1 "tg"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_1>; ccmm_props:ISO_639_2B "tgk"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_2B>; ccmm_props:ISO_639_2T "tgk"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_2T>; ccmm_props:ISO_639_3 "tgk"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3>; ccmm_props:XML_LNG "tg"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG>; a skos:Concept; skos:altLabel "tadjike"@fr, "tojikī"@tg, "тоҷикӣ"@tg; skos:definition "Tajik is a variety of modern Persian spoken in Central Asia. Most speakers of Tajik live in Tajikistan (where it is the official language) and Uzbekistan. In Afghanistan the dialect is less influenced by Turkic languages and is called Dari. The standard language is based on the north-western dialects of Tajik. Tajik also retains numerous archaic elements in its vocabulary, pronunciation, and grammar that have been lost elsewhere in the Persophone world, in part due to its relative isolation in the mountains of Central Asia.In Tajikistan and other countries of the former Soviet Union, Tajik Persian is currently written in Cyrillic script while the Perso-Arabic script is used in Iran and Afghanistan. The language is part of the Persian group within the southwestern branch of the Indo-Iranian languages."@en, "Le tadjik ou tadjike est une langue appartenant au groupe iranien de la famille des langues indo-européennes. Cette langue est proche du persan et du dari. Le tadjik est parlé en Asie centrale, principalement au Tadjikistan (dont il est la langue officielle), et par des minorités linguistiques en Afghanistan, en Iran, en Ouzbékistan et dans le Xinjiang, région autonome de Chine.Au Tadjikistan et en Ouzbékistan, la langue s’écrit au moyen d’un alphabet cyrillique augmenté de signes diacritiques. En revanche, dans les autres pays, elle est transcrite au moyen de l’alphabet perso-arabe. La langue fait partie du groupe persan dans la branche sud-ouest des langues indo-iraniennes."@fr; skos:exactMatch <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language/TGK>; skos:inScheme <https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages>; skos:prefLabel "таджикски"@bg, "tádžičtina"@cs, "tadsjikisk"@da, "Tadschikisch"@de, "τατζικικά"@el, "Tajik"@en, "tayiko"@es, "tadžiki keel"@et, "tadžikki"@fi, "tadjik"@fr, "Táidsícis"@ga, "tadžički"@hr, "tadźikšćina"@hsb, "tádzsik"@hu, "tadsjikíska"@is, "tagiko"@it, "tadžikų kalba"@lt, "tadžiku valoda"@lv, "таџикистански"@mk, "Taġik"@mt, "Tadzjieks"@nl, "tadsjikisk"@no, "tadżycki"@pl, "tajique"@pt, "tadjică"@ro, "таджикский"@ru, "tadžički"@sh, "tadžičtina"@sk, "tadžikistanščina"@sl, "таџички"@sr, "tadzjikiska"@sv, "tacikçe"@tr, "таджицька мова"@uk .
