<https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages/wny> ccmm_props:ISO_639_3 "wny"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3>; ccmm_props:XML_LNG "wny"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG>; a skos:Concept; skos:altLabel "Waanyi"@en, "Wanyi"@wny; skos:definition "Wanyi, also spelt Waanyi, is an endangered Australian Aboriginal language spoken by the Wanyi people of the lower gulf area of Northern Queensland. Although earlier thought to be extinct, as of the 2016 Australian census, there were 16 speakers of the language. Together with the closely related Garrwa language, Wanyi and Garrwa are not currently classified within any larger Aboriginal language family; instead they form their own Garrwan language family."@en; skos:exactMatch <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language/WNY>; skos:inScheme <https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages>; skos:prefLabel "Wanyi"@en .
<https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages/woa> ccmm_props:ISO_639_3 "woa"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#ISO_639_3>; ccmm_props:XML_LNG "woa"^^<http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XML_LNG>; a skos:Concept; skos:altLabel "Tyaraity"@en, "Kuwema"@woa; skos:definition "Kuwema, also known as Tyaraity, is a dormant Australian Aboriginal language traditionally spoken along the Daly River in the Northern Territory. Kuwema is related to Malak Malak. They were formerly classified as independent members of the Northern Daly languages, but are now considered a language isolate."@en; skos:exactMatch <http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/language/WOA>; skos:inScheme <https://nma.eosc.cz/vocabularies/languages>; skos:prefLabel "Kuwema"@en .
